@@ -0,0 +1,441 @@
+# Wave-Optics Diffraction Kernel (FFT glare) ??Specification
+
+Status: implemented, 2026-08-27 (pending user verification); revised the same day - half resolution,
+own layer, source pre-blur, no f-number gate in Wave mode. Owner of semantics: the camera manager.
+Branch: `glare` (off `dev` @ bc6e297c). Merged back to `dev` only after user verification.
+Predecessors: `DiffractionStreaksSpec.md` (the analytic streaks, which stay as a mode),
+`FilterPSFSpec.md` (the sparse PSF convolution this reuses), `DofLensModelSpec.md` 禮3.2
+(`LensPupil`, the pupil this diffracts).
+
+## 0. What this adds, and why now
+
+The analytic streaks paint N line pairs with an exponential falloff, a hue ramp and an f-number
+fade-in that was tuned by eye. They cannot show what a *real* aperture does: a 9-blade iris
+throws 18 rays with a specific intensity pattern, a star-shaped mask throws a pattern of its
+own, a rounded polygon (curved blades) softens and broadens the rays, and a slightly aberrated
+pupil puts halos around them. All of that is one statement: **the glare of a point source is
+the Fraunhofer diffraction pattern of the pupil, |F{P}|簡.** With `LensPupil` / the iris polygon
+/ the shape mask now defined in one place, the pupil is available; this round computes its
+diffraction pattern and convolves the frame's highlights with it, using the sparse sprite
+convolution the front filter already has.
+
+Physics reminders that shape the design:
+
+* The pattern's scale on the sensor is set by `弇 繚 N`: stopping down spreads the same energy
+  fraction over a proportionally larger pattern. Rays get longer *and dimmer per pixel* as N
+  grows; wide open they are short and hide under the source's own bloom. This IS the "sunstars
+  appear when you stop down" behaviour ??no hand-authored f-number ramp is needed or wanted.
+* Straight edges diffract into rays whose intensity falls as `1/u簡` along the edge normal
+  (edge diffraction); corners and smooth outlines fall faster (`1/u?循). The rays of a real
+  sunstar are visible for hundreds of pixels only because the source is 10糧??0?腕?brighter than
+  the scene; the pattern itself is fixed.
+* Each wavelength is a scaled copy: `PSF_弇(u) = (弇?/弇)簡 繚 PSF_弇?(u 繚 弇?/弇)`. Blue is compact,
+  red is stretched ??the rainbow along a ray is dispersion, not a colour ramp.
+* A DFT of the pupil sampled on an `L?L` grid with the pupil `d` texels across gives a PSF
+  whose texel pitch on the sensor is `弇 N 繚 d / L` ??for `L = 512, d = 256, 弇 = 550 nm, N = 11`
+  that is 3 繕m ??0.16 px at 1080p full frame, so the whole texture spans only ~40 px of
+  screen. **The FFT resolves the structure; the reach has to come from the analytic far-field
+  law.** That is the central design decision below (禮2.3).
+
+## 1. Modes and gating
+
+`Diffraction Streaks` group gains `Mode` ('stMd', enum): `Analytic` (today's line pairs,
+byte-identical behaviour) / `Wave` (this spec). **Default `Wave`.** Post param
+`HDR_Streaks_Mode` (0/1). Everything under the `Wave` branch is new code; the analytic passes
+are not touched. `r_HDRStreaks` stays the master switch (0 = neither mode; 2 = the analytic
+diagnostic force, unchanged, does not force Wave). New cvar `r_HDRDiffractionKernel` (int,
+default 1; 0 = kernel never built, Wave mode falls back to Analytic with one log line ??kill
+switch) and `r_HDRDiffractionDebug` (int, default 0; 1 = draw the kernel texture in the top-left
+corner at 4? with a log-scale tone curve, and log the rebuild parameters).
+
+## 2. Model
+
+### 2.1 The pupil raster
+
+On the CPU, a job builds the pupil amplitude `A(x)` on an `L ? L` complex grid, `L = 512`,
+pupil bounding diameter `d = L/2 = 256` texels (zero-padded ?2 so `|F|簡` ??the transform of the
+pupil's autocorrelation, which is twice as wide ??does not wrap). The pupil is the ON-AXIS pupil
+(no cat-eye: field-dependent clipping varies across the frame and a convolution kernel must be
+shift-invariant; the corners' clipped stars are a later refinement):
+
+```
+r, 庛 from the grid centre, unit radius = d/2
+polygon:  inside if r ??NGon_Rad(庛 ??phase, blades)^exponent          (same closed form and the SAME exponent the gather/splat use: 1 ??curvature 繚 p 繚 strength)
+mask:     ? mask(rotate(x, ?askRotation) 繚 maskScale)                 (R channel of the shape DDS, read through the DOF stage's existing CryPak DDS reader; N_mask / N scaling exactly as the splat's vDofBokehMask.y; 1 when no mask)
+barrel:   ? (r ??cos(?/n)^exponent / p)                                (the mechanical opening; no field offset on axis. p = N_open/N_geo = fPupilScale, 1 wide open)
+A(x)      = sqrt(polygon 繚 mask)  ?  exp(i 繚 2? 繚 W(x))                 (amplitude = sqrt of the intensity transmittance; W = wavefront error in waves)
+W(x)      = k_sa 繚 W_sa 繚 r??                                           (spherical aberration as a PHASE error; W_sa = 0.25 waves at |k_sa| = 1, cvar r_HDRDiffractionSAWaves, default 0.25. This is the physically right place for SA in the glare: an aberrated pupil puts rings and halos around the rays. Optional, 0 disables.)
+```
+
+**The barrel is what makes the rays appear when you stop down.** It is the fixed round opening
+the blades retract into, so it does not move with the iris: `p = N_open / N_geo` is the polygon's
+size divided by the barrel's, and the barrel's radius in the polygon's own units is therefore
+`(polygon size) / p`. `NGon_Rad` is normalised to the CIRCUMRADIUS, so "polygon size" ??the
+inscribed radius, where the blade edges actually are ??is `cos(?/n)^exponent`. Consequences:
+
+* `p = 1` (wide open): the barrel disc sits exactly on the blade edges and lies inside the
+  polygon everywhere else, so the intersection **is** the circle, tangent to the edges at their
+  midpoints. A circular pupil diffracts into rings. **No rays wide open** ??which is what a real
+  lens does, and the second half of "sunstars appear when you stop down" (the first half being
+  the `弇繚N` scale on the sensor). Curved blades (exponent < 1) are clipped by exactly the same
+  relative amount; at exponent 0 the polygon is already a circle and nothing is clipped.
+* `p ??cos(?/n)^exponent` (??0.92 for eight straight blades, 0.5 for three): the barrel is past
+  the corners and clips nothing at all. This is where a normal working aperture lives, and the
+  pupil there is the bare polygon ??unchanged from before this rule existed.
+* In between, the corners are cut back and the rays fade in continuously.
+
+The on-screen scale (`弇繚N繚d/L`, 禮2.3) is untouched by any of this: the polygon's size in the
+raster stays the unit and only the barrel moves.
+
+Edges are anti-aliased: evaluate 2?2 supersamples per texel (the polygon and barrel tests) and
+average; the mask is bilinearly sampled. The barrel applies to the mask as well ??the pupil is
+`mask ??polygon ??barrel`, and the mask's scale relative to the polygon is unchanged. A hard-edged pupil gives the sharp `1/u簡` rays; the
+splat's soft edge (`fEdgeSoft`) is a rendering convenience and is **not** applied here.
+
+### 2.2 The transform
+
+2-D FFT of `A`, `I(u) = |F{A}|簡`, DC at the centre (fftshift). Normalise so that `峉 I = 1`
+over the grid (Parseval: this is the total transmitted light). Then **remove the core**: the
+frame already contains the source itself (and its bloom), so the kernel must carry only the
+diffracted light. Zero every texel within `r_core = 2.5` Airy radii of the centre (`1.22 繚 L/d`
+texels per Airy radius ??~6 texels) and record the removed fraction `E_core` (log it; for a
+circular pupil it is ??0.9). What remains ??`I_glare`, integral `1 ??E_core` ??is the kernel.
+
+Store `I_glare` as a **prefiltered mip chain of the LINEAR intensity** in an `R16F` `L?L`
+texture with `log??L + 1` levels (`$DiffractionKernel`). The chain exists because the kernel's
+texel pitch on screen is a small *fraction* of a pixel (禮2.3): one deposit pixel covers
+~17 kernel texels at f/16 and ~100 wide open, while the rays inside the kernel are 1?? texels
+wide, so a single tap per pixel lands on a ray about one time in seventeen and the pattern
+disappears from the picture even though its *average* energy is right. Level `m` is built by
+box-filtering 2?2 of level `m??` **in linear intensity**, so `I` at every level is the mean
+intensity over that level's texel footprint.
+
+Encode `sqrt(I)` per level **after** the filtering, never before: `sqrt` is not linear, so a
+box filter of `sqrt(I)` is not `sqrt(mean I)` and the shader's `k簡` would come out
+systematically dark. The square root keeps the 10?領 tails above half-float denormals; the
+pixel shader squares it back, and because of the ordering `k簡` *is* the mean intensity over the
+sampled level's texel. Upload every level (the texture is created with the mip count and
+**without** `FT_NOMIPS`; both `GetOrCreate2DTexture`'s creation path and `UpdateTextureRegion`
+walk a source buffer whose levels are consecutive, which is how the job lays them out). Track the
+extrapolation boundary ring: `I_ring(庛)` = the mean of `I_glare` on the ring `r ??[R_tex ??4, R_tex]`
+(`R_tex = L/2 ??2` texels), 256 angular bins, stored in a second small texture
+`$DiffractionRing` (`256?1`, R16F, sqrt-encoded as well, **not** mipped ??256 bins over a
+~1600-texel circumference is already 6+ texels per bin, the same order as the angular footprint
+of a pixel covering 17 texels). The ring is what the far-field extrapolation samples.
+
+The removed core is left as it is: `r_core ??6` texels is 1.4 native pixels at f/16, so a
+coarse level averages the hole with its surroundings ??which is correct, because the hole sits
+at the source pixel and the frame carries the source there anyway.
+
+Implementation of the FFT: the engine already carries a radix-2 complex 1-D/2-D FFT in
+`Code/CryEngine/RenderDll/Common/WaterUtils.cpp` (`CWaterSim::ComputeFFT1D/2D`, "2 Dimensional
+FFT" by Paul Bourke). Lift that routine into a small standalone helper (`DiffractionKernel.cpp/.h`
+in the DOF stage's folder ??do not touch the water code) so the job has no dependency on the
+water simulation. `512簡` on one core is on the order of 10??0 ms: **never on the render
+thread.** The build runs as a `DECLARE_JOB` on the job system, double-buffered: the render thread
+keeps using the last finished kernel; when a job completes its result is uploaded to the
+texture on the render thread (`CTexture::UpdateTextureRegion` or recreate). At most one job in
+flight; if parameters change again while one runs, remember the newest request and start it
+when the current one finishes.
+
+Rebuild triggers (quantised so a smooth pull does not rebuild every frame; log each rebuild
+once): blades; exponent (steps of 0.02); phase (1簞); mask texture name; mask rotation (1簞);
+`N_mask/N` scale (steps of 0.05); `k_sa` (0.05); mode/cvar switches; **the pupil scale `p`**
+(steps of 0.02, but ONLY above `cos(?/n)^exponent ??0.02` ??below that the barrel cannot reach
+the polygon and every value gives the same pupil, so all of it collapses onto that one boundary
+bucket and an ordinary aperture sweep does not rebuild the kernel at all). Note that N itself does
+NOT trigger a rebuild ??the kernel is computed in the pupil's own units, and N enters only
+as the on-screen scale (禮2.3). The kernel therefore changes only when the pupil's *shape*
+changes.
+
+### 2.3 On-screen scale and the far-field extrapolation
+
+Kernel texel pitch on the sensor: `s = 弇? 繚 N_geo 繚 d / L` (metres; `弇? = 550 nm`, `N_geo` =
+the same geometric f-number the DOF uses, `max(N, N_mask)` when a mask is bound). In native
+pixels: `s_px = s 繚 W_px / W_sensor`. The texture covers `R_tex` texels, i.e. `R_tex 繚 s_px`
+pixels ??tens of pixels. The sprite is much larger (禮3); outside the texture the kernel is
+extrapolated by the edge-diffraction law:
+
+```
+u   = distance from the sprite centre, in kernel texels (after the 弇 scaling below)
+I(u, 庛) = I_tex(u, 庛)                              u ??R_tex
+        = I_ring(庛) 繚 (R_tex / u)簡                 u >  R_tex
+```
+
+The `1/u簡` law is exact for a straight edge and pessimistic for everything else, so the
+extrapolated tail is never brighter than the physics; a smooth (round) pupil, whose ring is
+already tiny, extrapolates to nothing. The energy in the extrapolated tail up to the sprite
+radius is `峉_庛 I_ring(庛) 繚 R_tex簡 繚 ln(R_sprite / R_tex)` ??finite. It is **not** normalised
+away: the kernel already carries physical units (禮2.4), so the tail is simply extra glare the
+sprite is or is not wide enough to hold. The CPU reports the coefficient so the split can be
+logged.
+
+Wavelength: the pixel shader samples at `n_弇 = 6` wavelengths (420, 470, 520, 570, 620, 670 nm),
+each a scaled copy `u_弇 = u 繚 弇? / 弇`, weight `(弇?/弇)簡`, colour from a small RGB table
+(approximate CIE ??sRGB, normalised so the six sum to white):
+
+| 弇 nm | R | G | B |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 420 | 0.05 | 0.00 | 0.35 |
+| 470 | 0.00 | 0.10 | 0.50 |
+| 520 | 0.00 | 0.55 | 0.15 |
+| 570 | 0.35 | 0.35 | 0.00 |
+| 620 | 0.45 | 0.00 | 0.00 |
+| 670 | 0.15 | 0.00 | 0.00 |
+
+(Columns are then rescaled on the CPU so `峉_弇 colour_弇 繚 (弇?/弇)簡 = (1,1,1)` ??white light stays
+white in the sum.) `Dispersion` ('stDs', 0..1, default 1) lerps every `弇` toward `弇?`: 0 = a
+monochrome white pattern at 550 nm.
+
+### 2.4 Energy and the source excess
+
+The front filter's sparse convolution (`FilterPSFSpec.md` 禮2??) run one resolution finer: the
+**half-res** HDR downsample (`m_pTexHDRTargetScaled[0][0]`, `$HDRTarget 1/2a`) thresholded with
+the streaks' own threshold (`HDR_Streaks_Threshold`, soft knee `T/4`) into a half-res scratch RT,
+**pre-blurred** (禮3), one sprite per surviving texel, deposit `E 繚 amount 繚 I(u)` additively into
+the glare's **own half-res layer** `$DiffractionLayer`, which is composited into the HDR target
+after the bloom stage. **No `1 / fBloomCompositeWeight`**: that factor existed only to undo the
+tone mapper's scaling of `$HDRFinalBloom`, and the layer does not travel that way. The on-screen
+energy is unchanged - `deposit 繚 (1/w) 繚 w` became `deposit`.
+
+`I` carries **physical units and is never renormalised**. The full pattern is scaled so
+`峉 I = 1` (Parseval ??the pupil's whole transmitted light) *before* the core is removed, so
+what the texture holds integrates to `1 ??E_core`, and the extrapolated tail adds
+`T_tail = 峉_庛 I_ring(庛) 繚 R_tex簡 繚 ln(R_sprite / R_tex)`. **`amount = 1` therefore deposits
+`E 繚 (1 ??E_core + T_tail)`, the physical fraction** ??about 0.07 of the source for an 8-blade
+iris, typically 5??5 % across pupils. Renormalising the core-removed kernel back to unit
+integral would be wrong twice over: it would hand the pattern the source's *entire* excess
+(~14? too much), and it would make the deposit depend on `R_sprite`, which is a cost choice.
+The only unit conversion the renderer applies is `1 / s_h簡`, turning "per kernel texel" into
+"per half-resolution pixel"; moving from quarter to half resolution is energy-neutral by
+construction (the pitch doubles, so the per-pixel deposit is quartered, and the sprite covers
+four times as many pixels). No f-number ramp on the component side in Wave mode - and, since the
+fix of 2026-08-27, no f-number **gate** either: `HDR_Streaks_Active` in Wave mode is
+`Enable Streaks && Streak Amount > 0.001`, with the analytic
+`pow(clamp((N??.3)/(11??.3)), 1.2)` ramp confined to Analytic mode where it belongs. It used to
+drive the flag in both modes, which switched the whole streak block off below f/6.3 and on hard
+at f/6.4 - a pop with no physics behind it. `HDR_Streaks_Threshold` keeps its meaning and
+default.
+
+**Visibility, and what `Streak Amount` really is.** That physical fraction is right and almost
+invisible, and the two facts are not in conflict. Of the `1 ??E_core` that leaves the source,
+nearly all sits in the first few Airy rings, under the source's own bloom; only about **0.5 %**
+of the source's light lands in the RAYS proper ??past ~20 Airy radii, where the eye reads a
+sunstar. Spread over 8 rays several hundred pixels long, that is on the order of **1e-5 of the
+source per pixel** twenty pixels out. A real sunstar is visible only because the sun, or a night
+streetlight against a dark street, is **1e4??e6? the rest of the scene**; a game light's HDR
+value is clamped to a few tens, so the same physics deposits nothing the display can show. The
+kernel build logs `E_far` (energy past 20 Airy radii) so this can be read per pupil.
+
+`Streak Amount` is therefore defined as a **source radiance boost**: how much brighter the real
+source is than the clamped HDR value standing in for it. `1` means "the HDR value IS the true
+radiance" ??physically exact, nearly invisible. `10??00` reproduces the star an actual lamp or
+sun of that kind would draw. Nothing about the pattern's **shape or falloff** changes with it ??+those stay physical at every value; only the source's assumed brightness does. Range `0..200`
+(slider `0..100`), **default 20**. Analytic mode ignores it.
+
+Anamorphic squeeze: the iris sits *behind* the cylindrical front group, so its diffraction
+pattern is formed unsqueezed on the sensor and the desqueeze stretches it horizontally by `S`:
+sprite x half-extent = `R 繚 S` (the opposite of the front filter, which sits in front of the
+cylinder). Check what the analytic streaks do with the squeeze today and note the difference
+in the code comment; do not change the analytic path.
+
+## 3. Renderer
+
+DOF stage (`DepthOfField.cpp/.h`) owns the kernel (it already owns the pupil description and
+the CPU mask reader): `CDiffractionKernel m_diffraction` with `Request(desc)` (quantise,
+compare, spawn job), `Update()` (called once per frame from `Execute`; uploads a finished job),
+`GetKernelTexture()`, `GetRingTexture()`, `GetNormalisation()`, `GetTexelPitchPupilUnits()`.
+The pupil description struct: blades, exponent, phase, mask texture name (or empty), mask
+rotation, mask scale, `k_sa`, `L`, `d`.
+
+Bloom stage (`Bloom.cpp`): `ExecuteDiffraction()` in the Wave branch of `ExecuteStreaks()`
+(same place in the frame: after bloom's downsamples, before the filter PSF and halation).
+
+**Half resolution, own layer, composited after bloom.** The pass owns three lazily allocated
+half-res `RGBA16F` targets (acquired / released / resized exactly like the halation targets, so
+Analytic mode and glare off cost nothing): `$DiffractionSource`, `$DiffractionSourceTemp` and
+`$DiffractionLayer`. The reason is that neither artefact of the quarter-res version was a
+windowing problem, and the windows of the previous round did not cure them:
+
+* **Source grid.** A lamp clears the threshold over a ~3?3 block of quarter-res texels, each of
+  which spawns its own sprite; the sum of nine offset copies of a radially symmetric pattern has
+  the *block's square* as its centre, whatever the pupil was.
+* **Deposit grid.** A quarter-res deposit is bilinearly magnified 4? onto a `1/u簡` slope that
+  crosses a whole texel in almost nothing - 4?4 native blocks.
+
+Half resolution halves both in each axis. On top of it the thresholded source gets a **separable
+5-tap Gaussian pre-blur**, ? ??1 half-res texel, weights normalised so the total excess is
+preserved (technique `HDRDiffractionSourceBlur`, two tiny passes). That is not smoothing: it is
+the convolution of the pattern with the source's own extent, which is what an extended source
+really does - and it is what turns the surviving block into a round blob.
+
+The chain is: threshold (`HDRStreakThreshold`, half-res HDR in, half-res scratch out, 1:1 now
+rather than the old 2:1 minification) ??blur H ??blur V ??clear the layer ??sprites into the
+layer ??`ExecuteDiffractionComposite()`. The composite is a sibling of the DOF's ghost composite,
+called from `CStandardGraphicsPipeline::ExecuteHDRPostProcessing` immediately before
+`CDepthOfFieldStage::ExecuteGhostComposite()`: an additive fullscreen blit
+(`GS_BLSRC_ONE | GS_BLDST_ONE`, `GS_NOCOLMASK_A`), bilinear from half res, technique
+`HDRDiffractionComposite`, consume-once through `m_bDiffractionLayerValid`. As with the ghosts,
+the layer lands **after** every glare threshold in the frame, so halation, the streaks and the
+front filter never see it - a diffraction pattern cannot itself halate. That is a deliberate
+asymmetry with the rest of the glare family and is correct.
+
+The sprite primitive is built like `m_passFilterPSF` (own per-view CB, now for the **half-res**
+grid, `SetCustomVertexStream(~0u,??`, `width_h 繚 height_h 繚 6` vertices, additive, alpha masked;
+the early quad collapse on a zero source stays, and matters more than before - four times as many
+candidate cells). New technique `HDRDiffractionPSF` in `HDRPostProcess.cfx`: VS = the filter
+PSF's VS with the x half-extent ? `S`, PS:
+
+```
+d        = quad-local in [-1, 1] 繚 R_sprite_px                       (half-res px from the sprite centre; x already carries the squeeze)
+for each 弇: fScale = lerp(1, 弇?/弇, dispersion)
+            u_弇 = |d| / s_h 繚 fScale;  庛 = atan2(d.y, d.x)
+            f_弇 = fScale / s_h                                      (kernel texels covered by ONE pixel at this 弇)
+            lod  = clamp(log??max(f_弇, 1)), 0, mips ??1)
+            I_弇 = (u_弇 ??R_tex) ? kernelLod(u_弇, 庛, lod)簡 : ring(庛)簡 繚 (R_tex / u_弇)簡
+            c  += colour_弇 繚 (弇?/弇)簡 繚 I_弇
+out = Color.rgb 繚 c 繚 norm 繚 w_in 繚 w_out                             (norm = 1 / s_h簡 only ??texel to pixel; the kernel is NOT renormalised; the windows below)
+```
+
+**The LOD is what makes the deposit an integral rather than a lottery.** `u = r 繚 fScale / s_h`,
+so one pixel of `r` is `f = fScale / s_h` texels of `u`; reading level `log??f` returns the mean
+intensity over the pixel's own footprint in one tap, and the surviving `1 / s_h簡` (the texel
+count in that footprint) turns that mean into the footprint's integral. The energy bookkeeping
+of 禮2.4 is unchanged ??what changes is that the *sampling* now integrates instead of point-
+testing 1 texel in 17. Never go below level 0 (`max(f, 1)`): the pattern can be magnified, in
+which case level 0 is already finer than a pixel.
+
+**The two screen-space windows.** The deposit is multiplied by `w_in 繚 w_out`, both functions
+of `r` (half-res pixels from the sprite centre) alone. Neither is decoration: each removes an
+artefact that is a property of the RASTERIZATION, not of the physics.
+
+* `w_out = 1 ??smoothstep(0.8, 1.0, r / R_sprite)`. The sprite is a square quad and the `1/u簡`
+  extrapolation is still finite at its boundary, so without a window the quad's edge is a hard
+  cut and every small emitter wears a **white square frame**. Feathering the last 20 % of the
+  radius and zeroing everything beyond it (the quad's corners, `r` up to `?? R`) makes the
+  cut-off round and invisible. Energy: it takes rather more than half of the last fifth of the
+  logarithmic tail, so the CPU's diagnostic tail estimate integrates to `0.9 R` ??+  `T_tail = c_tail 繚 ln(0.9 R_sprite / R_tex)`.
+* `w_in = smoothstep(0, 4, r)`, i.e. a fade-in over the first 4 **half-res** pixels ??the same
+  **8 native** the quarter-res version faded over 2 of its own, so the figure on screen is
+  unchanged. Just outside the removed core the pattern falls as `1/u簡` from about six kernel
+  texels ??1.4 native pixels ??a slope that crosses a whole deposit texel in almost nothing,
+  which showed as **4?4 native blocks** around the source at quarter resolution, 2?2 here.
+  (The analytic streaks never exposed this: their exponential falloff is orders of magnitude
+  gentler.) That radius already belongs to the source itself and to the bloom around it, so
+  fading the glare in over it costs nothing visible. It is **not** free in energy: it is the
+  steepest part of the pattern. The kernel's own core removal (2.5 Airy radii) is unchanged, so
+  the accounting of 禮2.4 stands and this is a small extra deficit at the very centre ??not
+  estimated, because it needs the kernel's radial profile, which lives on the job side; the pass
+  logs the window's radius in kernel texels instead.
+
+That prediction was tested and came true: the blocks survived the inner window, so the step named
+here ??"run the sprite pass into a half-resolution target of its own and composite that down" ??+has been taken, together with the source pre-blur the prediction missed (it read the deposit grid
+as the whole fault; the source grid was the other half).
+
+`kernelLod(u, 庛, lod)` samples `$DiffractionKernel` with `SampleLevel` at
+`0.5 + u/L 繚 (cos 庛, sin 庛)`; `ring(庛)` samples `$DiffractionRing` at `庛 / 2?` (bilinear, wrap).
+The kernel's sampler must be **`TrilinearClamp`** ??CRYENGINE builds `FILTER_LINEAR` samplers
+with `MaxLOD = 0`, which would silently pin every `SampleLevel` back to level 0 and undo the
+whole chain. Constants: `DiffractionParams0 = (R_sprite_px, s_h, R_tex, norm)`,
+`DiffractionParams1 = (dispersion, L, maxLod = mips ??1, r_HDRDiffractionDebug)`, and the six
+`colour_弇 繚 (弇?/弇)簡` as a `float4[6]` array (rgb + `弇?/弇`). The debug view
+(`r_HDRDiffractionDebug`) deliberately stays on level 0: it is there to show the kernel's true
+structure, not what a pixel averages out of it.
+
+`r_HDRDiffractionDebug 2` (shape only) replaces the deposit with a **decade map** of the
+spectral sum, `v = saturate((log10 I + 13) / 10)`. It cannot reuse the corner view's
+`log(1 + I繚10??`: that gain is calibrated for the kernel read at level 0, where a ray texel is
+around `1e-4`, whereas what reaches the sprite is the pixel's footprint MEAN (17 texels at f/16,
+100 wide open) plus the `1/u簡` tail ??`1e-4` down to `1e-12`, which the old curve mapped to
+0.0007, i.e. black. Mode 2 still applies both windows: they are geometry, and the mode exists to
+show the figure the pass actually rasterizes. `r_HDRDiffractionDebug 3` (the thresholded sources
+in the corner) reads the **half-res thresholded
+and pre-blurred** source ??what the sprite grid actually walks ??and takes a **5?5 max over the
+source's own texel grid**: the panel is a half-size copy of it, so it still minifies 2:1 and a one- or two-texel source is point-
+sampled away three times out of four ??the panel came up black with a lamp blazing in the frame.
+The max also dilates, so one hot texel paints a 3?3 block of panel pixels, and the panel carries
+a dim floor and a border so that "nothing cleared the threshold" and "the pass never ran" are not
+the same black rectangle.
+
+Sprite radius `R_sprite = Reach 繚 H_h` (half-res pixels; `Reach` ('stRc', 0..1, default 0.35,
+fraction of frame height, so the on-screen reach is unchanged) ??the cut-off of the extrapolated
+tail, purely a cost/coverage choice. At half resolution it is also **the** cost dial: the sprite
+is 2? wider in pixels and there are 4? as many candidate cells, so if a scene gets slow, `Streak
+Reach` is what to pull, and the log prints the sprite radius in half-res **and** native px. Cost
+target: < 0.3 ms GPU at 1080p with a few sources (each sprite pixel = 6 texture reads + 6 ring
+reads).
+
+Log once per change: `[BLOOM] diffraction kernel blades=%d exp=%.2f phase=%.0f mask=%s
+maskRot=%.0f maskScale=%.2f sa=%.2f -> core=%.2f tail=%.3f physical glare fraction = %.3f
+L=%d (job %.1f ms)` ??the fraction is `1 ??E_core`, so how much of the light really becomes
+glare is readable at a glance, plus one line for the mip chain (levels and size). The glare
+pass logs the fraction it actually deposits (tail included, `amount` applied) together with
+the texels a pixel covers and the LOD that follows from it at `弇?`, so a "no rays" report can
+be read straight off the log.
+
+## 4. Parameters
+
+Component `SCineStreakParams` (Diffraction Streaks): `Mode` (enum Analytic/Wave, default Wave),
+`Streak Amount` ('stAm', 0..200 with a 0..100 slider, default 50 ??a source radiance boost,
+禮2.4; Wave only ??in Analytic the f-number ramp still owns the amount), `Reach` ('stRc'), `Dispersion` ('stDs'). Existing `Blade Rotation`, `Enable
+Streaks`, threshold unchanged. Post params: `HDR_Streaks_Mode`, `HDR_Streaks_WaveAmount`,
+`HDR_Streaks_WaveReach`, `HDR_Streaks_Dispersion`; `SHDRStreaksParams` gains the four fields.
+Pupil description reaches the bloom stage through the DOF stage (`GetStage<CDepthOfFieldStage>()`),
+not through more post params: the DOF stage already resolves blades / exponent / phase / mask /
+mask rotation / mask scale / `k_sa` / `N_geo` every frame ??expose them from there.
+
+Cvars: `r_HDRDiffractionKernel` (1), `r_HDRDiffractionDebug` (0), `r_HDRDiffractionSAWaves`
+(0.25), `r_HDRDiffractionKernelSize` (512; 256 allowed for a 4? cheaper job at half the
+structural resolution ??same `d = L/2`).
+
+Schematyc `szName`s `[A-Za-z0-9_]+`; enum reflection as the other enums in the component.
+
+## 5. Limitations, stated up front
+
+* On-axis pupil only: the corners do not get their cat-eye-clipped stars. Later.
+* Shift-invariant: the pattern of a source is the same anywhere in the frame (true for the
+  iris; not for a vignetting barrel).
+* The `1/u簡` extrapolation is exact for straight edges only; rounded pupils fade faster than
+  the tail says ??but their ring is small, so the error is invisible.
+* The kernel is built on the CPU with a frame or two of latency after a shape change; during
+  a fast blade-rotation animation the pattern lags by that much. Acceptable; a compute-shader
+  FFT is the upgrade path.
+* Spectral sampling at six wavelengths shows slight banding in very long rays; raise `n_弇`
+  if the user sees it.
+
+## 6. Acceptance (user, by eye)
+
+1. Analytic mode: byte-identical to before (A/B).
+2. Wave mode, 8 blades, f/16, a lamp: 8 sharp rays with rainbow tails (blue near the core,
+   red at the tips), turning with Blade Rotation; f/8 ??shorter rays; f/2.8 ??nothing visible
+   beyond the bloom. No fade-in ramp needed.
+3. 3 blades ??6 rays; 9 blades ??18 rays (the analytic mode clamps at 24 line pairs; here
+   there is no clamp because it is not drawing lines).
+4. A shape mask (star / heart): the glare is the mask's own diffraction pattern, turning with
+   Mask Rotation; stopping the iris down through the mask (N > N_mask) changes it as the
+   polygon takes over.
+5. Blade Curvature 1 at f/2.8 (rounded polygon): rays soften and shorten compared with
+   curvature 0.
+6. Spherical Aberration 簣1 (with `r_HDRDiffractionSAWaves` 0.25): faint rings / halo around
+   the core of the pattern; 0 ??none.
+7. Dispersion 0: white rays.
+8. `Streak Amount` 1 reads as physically plausible against the lamp; 0 ??no pass;
+   `r_HDRDiffractionKernel 0` ??analytic fallback, one log line.
+9. RenderDoc: sprite pass < 0.3 ms at 1080p with a few sources; kernel job time in the log;
+   no hitch on the render thread when the pupil changes.
+
+## 7. Docs
