+
+---
+
+## Viewfinder overlays
+
+**Frame guides** are monitor markings, so they are drawn in viewport space, not in picture
+space: they do not squeeze with an anamorphic presentation and they do not bend with lens
+distortion - a guide that moved with the image would stop telling the operator where the edges
+of the frame are. The aspect guide is different from the letterbox: the letterbox is part of
+the picture (a gate mask that is recorded), the aspect guide only outlines the band of the
+target aspect and shades what falls outside it, so a 2.39 composition can be judged inside a
+16:9 viewport without committing to the crop.
+
+**Focus peaking** tints edges that are inside the depth of field. The in-focus band is exactly
+this camera's thin-lens depth of field (`ComputeDOFBounds`, the same near/far pair the DOF
+model uses), with a 5% soft falloff on either limit, so a focus pull moves the tinted region
+with the focus - the near and far limits are re-pushed every frame. Edge response is the
+largest absolute luminance difference to the four neighbours of the final image, so what is
+tinted is what actually reads as sharp on screen, not a depth threshold.
+
+**Guide legend.** While the guides are on, the component prints two small white labels over
+the frame (same opacity as the lines): the aspect of the framing guide, just inside the top
+left corner of its band, and a framing readout in the bottom left -
+`50mm  f/2.8  focus 3.20m  Full Frame`. The stop switches to the T-stop
+(`T2.9`) as soon as the glass transmits less than all of the light, because that is the
+number a lens is actually pulled by. These are aux-text overlays and follow the same kill
+switch as the guides.
+
+**Diagnosing peaking.** `r_LensPeakingDebug 1` paints the in-focus band as a solid tint with
+the edge response dropped, so the band itself is visible; `r_LensPeakingDebug 2` paints the
+scene depth as a grey ramp (black at the camera, white at twice the far limit) with the band
+tinted on top. Both need `r_LensPeaking` above 0. If mode 1 shows a band in the right place
+but normal peaking shows nothing, the depth is fine and the picture simply has no edges
+there; if mode 2 is flat, the depth read is what is broken.
+
+**Recording safety.** Everything runs at the very end of the PostAA composite, after grain and
+sharpening, so the overlays are clean of both; nothing upstream of them is touched. They are
+driven by `r_LensOverlays`, which the component sets to 1 only while editing (or when
+**Overlays In Game Mode** is on) and to 0 otherwise, and which can be set to 0 by hand as a
+global kill switch. With the overlays off the composite is bit-for-bit the image it produced
+before this feature existed.
+
+---
+
+## Exposure modes
+
+Only active with `Enable Exposure Control` on. The component default is **Auto + Bias
+(game)**.
+
+### Manual (physical)
+
+The standard photographic equation gives EV100, and the tone mapper's `[EV Min, EV Max]`
+window is collapsed onto a single value ??exposure comes entirely from the lens, which is the
+only way the f-number can change brightness:
+
+```
+EV100   = log2(N^2 / (transmission * t)) - log2(ISO / 100) - EVCompensation + NDstops
+applied = NeutralEV + (EV100 - NeutralEV) * ExposureResponse
+```
+
+`applied` is clamped to [-6, 24] and written to both EV Min and EV Max. Exposure Response 1
+restores strict physics; the default 0.6 mimics the S curve a camera's image processing rolls
+the extremes off with.
+
+### Auto + Bias (game) ??AutoBiased
+
+Keeps the scene's own adaptation window and shifts it by the lens's **relative** stops:
+
+```
+bias           = (EV100 - NeutralEV) * ExposureResponse
+EV Min/EV Max  = scene value + bias (clamped -6..24); the auto compensation term is untouched
+```
+
+The engine's night is artist-lit and only a few stops darker than its day, so an absolute EV
+lock blows out night scenes; this mode lets auto exposure keep tracking the scene while the
+lens still visibly biases it. The base window tracks external (TOD) writes so the shift never
+compounds frame by frame.
+
+### Auto Compensation (legacy)
+
+Writes only `E3DPARAM_HDR_EYEADAPTATION_PARAMS.z`, the scene-key auto compensation strength.
+The engine scales that term by how far the scene is from the reference key, so its sign flips
+between bright and dark scenes and the aperture cannot affect brightness through it. Kept for
+compatibility with older setups.
+
+---
+
+## T-stop: radiometry on T, geometry on f
+
+`Transmission` defines the T-stop:
+
+```
+T = f / sqrt(transmission)
+```
+
+**Radiometry (exposure, the grain light term) follows T** ??glass losses darken the image
+exactly like a real lens ??while **geometry (DOF, bokeh size and shape, diffraction) stays on
+the f-number**. Whenever the lens changes, the console logs the effective T-stop
+(`[CinematicCamera] f/2.8 at transmission 0.81 -> T3.1`).
+
+---
+
+## Depth of field and bokeh
+
+### Thin lens
+
+```
+CoC limit c = sensorDiagonal(mm) / 1500 / 1000    (metres)
+near = d*f^2 / (f^2 + N*c*(d-f))
+far  = d*f^2 / (f^2 - N*c*(d-f))     (denominator <= 0 -> infinity)
+```
+
+### Lens model depth of field
+
+With **Lens Model DOF** on (the default), the renderer stops being handed a focus *range* and
+is handed a lens. The signed circle of confusion is the thin lens, straight:
+
+```
+c_mm(d) = f^2 / (N * (d_f - f)) * (d - d_f) / d          signed DIAMETER on the sensor
+c_px    = c_mm * frameWidth_px / sensorWidth_mm
+r_unit  = 0.5 * c_px / U      U = 4 * max(W/1920, H/1080) native px of disc RADIUS per unit
+```
+
+Positive is behind focus (far layer), negative in front (near layer) - the near/far split falls
+out of the sign instead of being two separately authored ramps. `N` is the *geometric* aperture,
+so a shape mask in front of the lens shrinks the disc exactly as an iris does. Field curvature
+bends `d` before the formula and edge softness floors the far side after it, both unchanged in
+meaning. `Focus Falloff`, the derived focus range and the derived blur amount are not used
+(they keep being sent, so switching the model off lands back on exactly the stock path).
+
+Four consequences you can see:
+
+- **The depth of field is native.** The composite holds the sharp frame until the disc is
+  larger than the pixel it would blur (`beta = saturate((r_px - 0.5) / 1.5)`), instead of
+  lerping a half-resolution blur in the moment the CoC leaves zero.
+- **Bokeh has energy.** Each gather tap is weighted by `coverage x 1/CoC^2 x pupil` - the light
+  one point actually scattered onto this pixel - so a highlight going further out of focus
+  widens *and dims* rather than becoming a flat plate, and the sprite splat becomes a
+  refinement of the same quantity instead of a second energy convention bolted on top.
+- **Foreground edges are translucent.** The near layer's opacity is the pupil-weighted fraction
+  of rays that landed on the foreground, which is what partial occlusion physically is.
+- **Hyperfocal behaviour works.** Focusing far genuinely leaves the background sharp, which the
+  stock quadratic ramp cannot express at all.
+
+The two caps are not the same kind of thing, and it matters. The circle of confusion the
+renderer writes and every consumer downstream reads - the tile pass, the layer alpha, above all
+the **sprite's size** - is the *physical* one, clipped only by
+`r_DepthOfFieldLensModelSplatMaxCoC` (default 256, a native-pixel **diameter**, so 128 px of
+radius). `r_DepthOfFieldLensModelMaxCoC` (default 4 units, ~16 px radius at 1080p) is not a cap
+on the lens at all: it is how far the 121-tap half-resolution **gather kernel** is allowed to
+reach, applied inside the gather on its own copies of the number. So a lamp well behind focus
+draws its sprite at the full physical size and carries most of the light, and the gather's
+capped disc sits underneath it as a fainter, smaller underlay.
+
+`r_DepthOfFieldLensModelJitter` (default 1) offsets every gather tap inside its own cell by a
+per-pixel amount, clamped back inside the iris polygon so the silhouette's outline stays exact.
+Without it a fixed tap grid samples the source at the same 121 places in every pixel and a large
+disc comes apart into visible copies of the source; with it (and TSAA resolving the noise) a
+gather cap of ~8 is usable. Set it to 0 to see the tap copies again for comparison.
+`r_DepthOfFieldLensModel 0` disables the whole thing from the console;
+`r_DepthOfFieldLensModelDebug 1` logs the uncapped circle of confusion once a second and paints
+the composite as a false-colour map. Full model in [DofLensModelSpec.md](DofLensModelSpec.md).
+
+**Spherical Aberration** is the one lens property this round adds, and it exists because there is
+now a single pupil function every consumer evaluates. It is an *apodization*:
+`w(r) = 1 + side * k * (2r^2 - 1)` across the pupil, which integrates to zero over the unit disc,
+so the disc keeps its total energy and only redistributes it. Positive `k` piles the light at the
+rim behind focus and smears it into the middle in front (over-corrected, soap-bubble bokeh);
+negative `k` is the reverse, the STF / DC apodized look. The gather halo, the sprite splats and
+the ghosts all read the same `k`, so they cannot disagree about where the light in a disc sits.
+
+### Focus Falloff
+
+**Stock path only** (`Lens Model DOF` off). The DOF shader has no sharp band; it computes
+
+```hlsl
+coc = saturate(|depth - focusDistance| / (focusRange * 0.5))^2 * blurAmount
+```
+
+so feeding it the physical depth of field width puts *full* blur exactly where the image
+should still be acceptably sharp. The component feeds `dofWidth * FocusFalloff` instead,
+which puts the physical DOF limits at `1/falloff^2` of maximum blur (11% at the default 3):
+the depth of field reads as sharp and the falloff beyond it stays smooth.
+
+### Blur amount
+
+**Stock path only** (`Lens Model DOF` off).
+
+```
+blurAmount = clamp(focalLength / max(N, 0.7) / 60, 0, 4)    // reference 85mm f/1.4 ~ 60mm
+```
+
+Deliberately not clamped at 1.0: long lenses wide open produce genuinely larger discs, but
+the gather kernel is still 49 taps, so watch for sparse/ringy discs at the top end.
+
+### Bokeh polygon
+
+The component writes `Dof_User_ApertureBlades`, `Dof_User_FNumber`, `Dof_User_BladeCurvature`
+and `Dof_User_BladeRotation` every frame. The renderer's kernel crosses from a circle to the
+blade polygon **continuously with the aperture**, because that is what the blades actually do:
+a curved blade bounds the opening with a circular arc, and how far that arc bulges away from
+the polygon's chord is the ratio of the opening radius to the blade's own (fixed) arc radius.
+Wide open the outline is round; stopped down the same arc reads as a straight chord. The
+opening radius relative to wide open is the pupil scale `p` the field-pupil code already
+carries, so the shaping exponent is
+
+```
+exponent = clamp(1 - BladeCurvature * p * r_DepthOfFieldBokehShapeStrength, 0, 1)
+```
+
+with 0 = a perfect circle and 1 = the exact polygon. Straight blades (curvature 0) have no arc
+and give the exact polygon at every aperture; fully curved blades are a circle wide open, half
+way to the polygon one stop down, three quarters two stops down. There is no f-stop window any
+more - the old f/1.2 - f/2.8 blend was an approximation of exactly this curve for an f/1.2
+lens, and it wrongly pinned every lens to the exact polygon above f/2.8, which made Blade
+Curvature do nothing to the outline at ordinary working apertures.
+
+**The polygon never exceeds the true iris.** A real aperture is the intersection of its blades,
+so it is always convex; the shaping exponent is therefore capped at 1 (= exactly the n-gon),
+with 0 being a perfect circle. `r_DepthOfFieldBokehShapeStrength` (default 1.0) scales the
+**rounding** term above - the reach of the blade curvature, not a "polygon strength": above 1
+curved blades stay round further down the stops, below 1 they sharpen into the polygon sooner.
+Neither direction can push past the true polygon. It used to run up to 4, which
+stretched the corners outward - harmless-looking at 8 blades, where an octagon's corners sit
+only 8% past its edge midpoints, but catastrophic at 3, where a triangle's corners sit 2.0x out
+and squaring that turned the bokeh into a concave three-lobed cloverleaf.
+
+Blade curvature's second role is the **edge**: a curved blade
+presents a slightly soft, rounded outline and a straight blade cuts a crisp one, so the splat's
+polygon edge width runs from 0.06 (curvature 1) to 0.015 (curvature 0) in pupil-radius units.
+Same silhouette either way, only how hard it is cut.
+
+`r_DepthOfFieldBokehQuality N` (N >= 3) forces an N-sided kernel for diagnostics. See
+[docs/ConsoleReference.md](docs/ConsoleReference.md).
+
+### Sprite bokeh (hybrid highlights)
+
+**Use Sprite Bokeh** switches `r_DepthOfFieldMode` to 0, the hybrid path: the full gather blur
+runs exactly as in mode 1, and a splat layer is added on top for out-of-focus **highlights**
+only. A pixel splats when its scene HDR luminance exceeds **Highlight Threshold** (soft knee
+of +/-50% around it), and each splat deposits energy scaled by **Highlight Splat Gain**. A
+scene with no bright highlights therefore looks the same in either mode. The cvar is saved and
+restored like the motion blur cvars, so leaving the camera never strands the renderer in a
+mode nobody asked for.
+
+### Shape mask and Mask Aperture
+
+**Bokeh Shape Texture** is the silhouette (red channel) of a physical mask mounted in front of
+the lens, pushed to the `Dof_User_BokehShapeTex` post param. It only does anything while the
+hybrid splats are running, and it is rewritten every frame on purpose: the param texture keeps
+one slot per render command buffer, and a name written once reaches only one of them.
+
+Two separate aperture mechanisms - the mask's value field and the blade polygon - combined as a
+**physical intersection**: the pupil is `mask ??iris` at every f-number, with
+`k = Mask Aperture / f-number` the iris radius in mask units.
+
+- **k > 1** (iris opened wider than the mask): the mask bounds the pupil; the polygon only trims
+  whatever of the silhouette pokes past it (the outermost corners first). The gather taps are
+  importance-sampled from the same clipped pupil, so even the soft halo takes the silhouette.
+- **k < 1** (iris stopped down past the mask): the polygon bounds the pupil and the mask is
+  sampled at that scale, so the silhouette is clipped corner by corner and rounded off
+  progressively until the iris sits inside the cut-out's open centre and the plain polygon
+  remains. The fill style (radial iris look vs mask value field) follows whichever hole is the
+  smaller one.
+
+The pupil is rebuilt on the CPU in quantized iris steps (the same 64x64 mask pixels the gather
+kernel samples), and its fill fraction of the splat quad drives the energy gain exactly - a
+thin cut-out deposits as much light as the disc it replaces at any f-number.
+
+The mask is an optical element, not just a shape. The thin-lens depth of field and the maximum
+blur follow the pupil's bounding circle, `max(f-number, Mask Aperture)`
+(`GetGeometricAperture()`), so a note cut-out at f/2 in front of an f/1.2 iris produces
+f/2-sized discs; and the light reaching the sensor is `area(iris ??mask) / area(iris)`,
+integrated from the mask texture for the current f-number, times **Mask Open Area** (tint),
+through the T-stop transmission term - so the exposure modes and the film grain light term
+react to the partly blocked hole exactly like a neutral density loss, continuously. The
+renderer still receives the raw iris f-number. The T-stop log line reports the effective
+numbers: `f/1.2 with mask f/2.0 (iris/mask radius 1.67, tint 100%) -> effective transmission
+0.21, T2.6, CoC from f/2.0`.
+
+The renderer compensates a textured splat for how little of its bounding square a mask passes
+(~0.35 for the engine's own bokeh masks), so a star reads as present as the circle it
+replaces. Domain and shape-texture transitions are logged once per change (`[DOF] gather
+domain=...`, `[DOF] shape texture '...' -> resolved ...`).
+
+### Field-dependent pupil (cat-eye, coma, astigmatism)
+
+A ray reaching the corner of the frame does not look at the lens straight down the axis: it
+passes the barrel and the element rims obliquely, so the pupil it sees is **clipped by the
+mechanical opening** - more the further out it is. **Cat-eye** turns that one fact on, and it
+produces corner bokeh cut into lens shapes (long axis tangential), darker edges, dimmer edge
+bokeh, and all of it fading as the iris closes, at once.
+
+The pupil's size relative to the barrel is `Maximum Aperture / geometric f-number`, so nothing
+here needs a separate aperture link: stop down from Maximum Aperture and the pupil simply stops
+reaching the barrel. **Coma** (radial comet tails, proportional to the square of the pupil
+radius) rides the same plumbing and vanishes the same way; **Astigmatism** splits the radial and
+tangential foci, so off-axis blur stretches radially on one side of focus and tangentially on
+the other, and being a focus shift it is aperture independent.
+
+The mechanical light loss is applied **exactly once**, in the tonemap vignette - it belongs to
+in-focus pixels just as much as to blurred ones. The gather only reshapes its kernel (its
+renormalisation keeps the brightness) and the splat divides the loss back out of its deposit, so
+sprite and halo agree at the frame edges. Tuning: `r_DepthOfFieldCatEyeBarrel`,
+`r_DepthOfFieldCatEyeReach`; diagnostic: `r_DepthOfFieldFieldDebug 1` paints the transmission as
+a grey ramp. Full model in [FieldPupilSpec.md](FieldPupilSpec.md).
+
+### Lens flaws (field curvature, edge softness, chromatic aberration)
+
+The field-dependent pupil describes what a cheap lens does where it is *out* of focus. It is
+also bad where it is *in* focus, and three more properties say how. **Field Curvature** curves
+the focal surface: with the centre in focus the edges focus at a different distance, so a flat
+wall goes soft toward the edges (positive = the edges focus closer, the usual inward curvature).
+**Edge Softness** is the residual off-axis aberration that blurs an edge which is genuinely in
+focus; it goes with the square of the pupil radius, so it is at its worst at Maximum Aperture
+and gone stopped down. **Chromatic Aberration** is lateral colour fringing: red and blue are
+magnified slightly differently from green, so high-contrast edges pick up red/cyan fringes that
+grow with the distance from the centre.
+
+None of the three costs a render pass. The first two are focus errors, so they live inside the
+depth of field's own circle of confusion - field curvature scales the depth the focus math is
+fed, edge softness is a floor on the FAR layer's circle of confusion (an in-focus edge then
+blends behind the near layer the way a blurred background does). Chromatic aberration is
+distortion done one wavelength at a time, so it lives in the final composite's coordinate chain
+next to Distortion and the anamorphic squeeze, as two extra samples driven by
+`r_LensChromaticAberration` and pushed and restored exactly like `r_LensDistortion`. Full model
+in [LensFlawsSpec.md](LensFlawsSpec.md).
+
+### Axial chromatic aberration
+
+Chromatic Aberration above is a *magnification* error and shows at the edges of the frame.
+**Axial Chromatic Aberration** is the other half: a *focus* error, each wavelength landing on
+its own plane, so every out-of-focus disc is a different size per channel. Highlights behind
+the focus plane come back with a green rim, highlights in front of it with a magenta one, and
+because it has nothing to do with field height the centre of the frame fringes as hard as the
+corners. This is the bokeh fringing of fast and vintage glass; being a focus error, stopping
+down takes it away with everything else.
+
+No extra pass and no extra samples: the gather keeps one kernel at the layer's circle of
+confusion and multiplies each tap by a per-channel radial mask, so a channel whose disc is
+smaller only accepts the taps inside its own radius, and each channel is then normalised over
+the weight it actually took. The sprite splat draws the same pupil twice, once at full radius
+and once shrunk, and composes the layer's colour from the two - so halo and sprite agree on
+where the coloured rim starts. Full model in
+[AxialChromaticSpec.md](AxialChromaticSpec.md).
+
