+
+## Diffraction streak physics
+
+The glare of a point source is one statement: **it is the Fraunhofer diffraction pattern of the
+pupil the light passed through**, `|F{A}|^2`. Everything a sunstar does follows from that, and
+the camera offers two models of it ??`Streak Model`, exclusive, defaulting to Wave.
+
+### Wave optics (default)
+
+The renderer rasters the **on-axis pupil** on a 512x512 complex grid ??the iris polygon (the
+same `NGon_Rad` outline and the same curvature-derived exponent the bokeh uses), intersected
+with the shape mask at the mask's own rotation and scale, intersected with the barrel ??and
+gives it a phase: spherical aberration is a **wavefront error**, `W = k_sa * 0.25 waves * r^4`,
+which is the physically right place for it and is what puts rings and a halo around the rays.
+An FFT of that is the point spread function. The **core is removed** (the frame already carries
+the source and its bloom) and the frame's thresholded highlights are convolved with what is
+left ??one sprite per bright **half-resolution** texel, the front filter's sparse convolution run
+one resolution finer and deposited into a **layer of its own**, which is added to the frame after
+the bloom stage (where the lens ghosts are added, and for the same reason: every glare threshold
+has already been taken, so the pattern cannot halate or streak off itself). The thresholded
+source is given a small Gaussian pre-blur first ??physically the convolution of the pattern with
+the source's own extent, practically what stops a lamp's block of bright texels from summing into
+a pattern with a **square** core. Quarter resolution did both of those things wrong: the source
+block was 3x3 and the deposit was magnified 4x onto the pattern's steepest slope, which is what
+put visible blocks around every source. The pattern is scaled to the pupil's whole transmitted light *before* the core
+comes out and is never renormalised afterwards, so **`Streak Amount` 1 deposits exactly the
+physical fraction** ??of which only about **0.5 % of the source** reaches the rays proper,
+some `1e-5` of the source per pixel twenty pixels out. Real sunstars are visible only because
+the sun or a night streetlight is `1e4`?1e6`? the scene, while a game light's HDR value is
+clamped to a few tens; so **`Streak Amount` is a source radiance boost** ??how much brighter
+the true source is than that clamped value ??with **50** as the default and 10??00 the useful
+range (entities saved before that default changed keep their saved value). It scales the
+source, never the pattern: shape and falloff stay physical throughout.
+
+What that buys, none of it authored:
+
+- **Ray count is the pupil's, not a formula.** 8 blades -> 8 rays, 3 -> 6, 9 -> 18, with the
+  real relative intensities. There is no clamp, because nothing is drawing lines. More blades
+  therefore means THINNER and FAINTER rays, not a brighter star: the same diffracted energy is
+  divided among more of them (energy per ray ~ 1/N, and an odd blade count makes 2N of them).
+  That is the physics, not a shortfall.
+- **No f-number ramp, and none wanted.** The pattern's size on the sensor is
+  `s = lambda * N * d / L`: stopping down spreads the SAME energy fraction over a proportionally
+  larger pattern. Rays get longer and dimmer per pixel as N grows, and wide open they are short
+  enough to hide under the source's own bloom. That IS "sunstars appear when you stop down".
+  The component's Analytic f-number ramp does not gate this model either ??in Wave mode the pass
+  is live at every aperture, so nothing pops in at f/6.4.
+- **Wide open there are no rays at all, because there is no polygon.** The blades retract into
+  the round barrel, and at `Maximum Aperture` they are flush with it: the pupil is the barrel's
+  circle, and a circle diffracts into rings. The iris polygon only emerges from the barrel as
+  you stop down (below `p = N_open/N ??cos(pi/n)` it is clear of it entirely), and the rays come
+  out with it. That is the other half of "sunstars appear when you stop down".
+- **Blade Curvature shortens and softens the rays** ??a rounded outline has less straight edge
+  to diffract off ??and **a shape mask throws its own pattern**, turning with `Mask Rotation`.
+- **The rainbow is dispersion.** Six wavelengths (420-670 nm), each a scaled copy
+  `PSF_l(u) = (l0/l)^2 PSF_l0(u l0/l)`: blue compact near the core, red out at the tips.
+  `Streak Dispersion` collapses them onto 550 nm for a white pattern.
+
+The FFT resolves the pattern's **structure** but not its **reach** ??the whole texture spans
+only some tens of pixels on screen. Past its edge the kernel is extrapolated by the
+edge-diffraction law `I_ring(theta) * (R_tex / u)^2`, exact for a straight edge and pessimistic
+for anything smoother, so the tail is never brighter than the physics allows. `Streak Reach` is
+where that tail is cut off ??and, since the pass moved to half resolution, it is also **the cost
+dial**: the sprite is twice as wide in pixels as it was and there are four times as many
+candidate source cells, so if a scene gets slow, pull `Streak Reach` first.
+
+The kernel depends only on the pupil's **shape**, so it is rebuilt on a **job** (10-30 ms, never
+on the render thread, one in flight, the previous kernel stays live meanwhile) and only when
+blades, the polygon exponent, either rotation, the mask, the mask scale or the spherical
+aberration actually change. The f-number does **not** trigger a rebuild; it only scales the
+result. Each rebuild logs its parameters, the core fraction, the physical glare fraction
+(`1 - core`) and the job's time; the glare pass logs the fraction it actually deposits.
+
+Known limits: the pupil is the **on-axis** one, so the corners do not get their cat-eye-clipped
+stars; the convolution is shift-invariant; and a shape change takes a frame or two to appear.
+The kernel lives in the depth of field stage (that is where the pupil is resolved), so Wave
+needs `r_dof > 0`; until the first kernel lands the analytic lines carry the effect. Full model
+in [DiffractionKernelSpec.md](DiffractionKernelSpec.md).
+
+Cvars: `r_HDRDiffractionKernel` (0 = kill switch, falls back to Analytic),
+`r_HDRDiffractionDebug`, `r_HDRDiffractionKernelSize`, `r_HDRDiffractionSAWaves`.
+
+### Analytic (the older model, kept)
+
+Light bends around the straight edge of every iris blade, and each edge throws a pair of rays:
+
+- **Even blade count**: opposing rays overlap -> **N rays**. **Odd count**: offset -> **2N**.
+  The ray count is clamped to [4, 24] (the renderer draws them as 12 symmetric line passes at
+  most, each painting two opposed spikes).
+- Brightness: `amount = ((N - 6.3) / (11 - 6.3))^1.2`, clamped 0-1 ??nothing below f/6.3,
+  **saturated by f/11**, after which stopping down reads as the rays growing rather than
+  brightening.
+- Length: `length = (N - 7) / (22 - 7)`, clamped 0-1 ??the **length curve owns f/7 to f/22**.
+- `Blade Rotation` goes straight into `HDR_Streaks_Angle` (and into the bokeh polygon's
+  rotation).
+
+These are the `HDR_Streaks_*` post effect params; an unmodified renderer does not know them
+(reads return 0, writes are silently dropped), so the effect needs this renderer/shader set.
+The ramp and the falloff here were tuned by eye; they are an approximation of what the wave
+model computes. Byte for byte unchanged, and still what `r_HDRStreaks 2` forces.
+
+Master cvar: `r_HDRStreaks` (2 = diagnostic force mode, analytic only).
+
