+
+## Halation
+
+On film, light from a very bright source does not stop at the emulsion. It passes through,
+reflects off the film base and re-exposes the emulsion from behind, in a halo far wider than
+the source itself. In a colour negative the red-sensitive layer sits deepest, so what comes
+back is red-orange, its edge is soft, and it only appears around sources that genuinely
+**overload** the emulsion - never around a merely bright sky.
+
+Halation is a property of the **film stock**, not of the lens. Unlike the diffraction streaks
+and the anamorphic flare, nothing here is modulated by the f-number: the four properties are
+pushed to the renderer exactly as authored, every frame, the same way the streak params are.
+
+The pass chain lives in `CBloomStage::ExecuteHalation()`, in the **bloom domain** - it runs
+right after the streaks, reuses the two quarter-resolution HDR scratch targets they leave
+free, and blends into `$HDRFinalBloom` with the same bloom-weight compensation, so it costs no
+permanent memory and needs no second tone-map hook:
+
+1. **Threshold** the half-res HDR scene at `Halation Threshold` with a soft knee of a quarter
+   of the threshold (the `HDRStreakThreshold` technique).
+2. **Spread** it wide, horizontally then vertically, through `HDRStreakStretch` with an
+   isotropic direction, a stride of `2 + 10 * radius` quarter-res texels and a near-flat per
+   tap decay of 0.90. This is what sets the halo's width: at radius 0.5 the 7 taps per side
+   reach 49 texels, about 196 full-res pixels on a 1080p frame.
+3. **Round** the profile with two `CGaussianBlurPass` passes at distribution `3 + 5 * radius`.
+   That utility's 16 bilinear taps are pinned to +-8 texels whatever the distribution, so it
+   cannot widen anything - it only smears the discrete tap gaps into a continuous soft edge.
+4. **Composite** into the bloom target with the stretch shader at a **zero** step (every tap
+   lands on the same texel, so the normalised sum is a plain tinted sample), tinted by
+   `Halation Tint` and scaled by `amount * 8 / bloomCompositeWeight`. The x8 gain is there
+   because an energy-conserving blur spreads a source over thousands of texels: without it the
+   halo is physically right and visually invisible.
+
+These are the `HDR_Halation_*` post effect params; an unmodified renderer does not know them
+(reads return 0, writes are silently dropped), so the effect needs this renderer/shader set.
+Master cvar: `r_HDRHalation` (**2 = diagnostic force mode**: amount 1, threshold 0.5, radius
+0.7, independent of the game-side params).
+
+---
+
+## Front filter PSF
+
+A cross-screen / star filter is a plate of etched grooves mounted in **front** of the lens.
+Every bright point in the frame is diffracted into lines perpendicular to the grooves: 4, 6 or
+8 points depending on the plate, at whatever angle the plate is turned to, at **every**
+f-number - unlike the iris sunstars, which need a stopped-down aperture - with rainbow
+dispersion toward the line ends. Brighter sources show longer lines not because the pattern
+changes, but because more of its faint tail rises above the scene.
+
+That last sentence is the whole model: a front filter is a fixed **point spread function**,
+and the picture is the scene convolved with it. The PSF is authored as an RGB texture, the way
+the bokeh shape mask is, so the same mechanism covers any other front-element PSF a preset may
+want (a smeared or dirty filter, a custom flare plate); the star filter is only its first user.
+With nothing bound, a procedural N-point star with rainbow tips stands in, so the effect is
+testable and has a sane default.
+
+The convolution is done **sparsely**, in `CBloomStage::ExecuteFilterPSF()`, right after the
+streaks and in the same window where both quarter-resolution HDR scratch targets are free:
+
+1. **Threshold** the half-res HDR scene at `Star Threshold` with a soft knee of a quarter of
+   the threshold (the `HDRStreakThreshold` technique the streaks and the halation share).
+   Skipped entirely at amount 0 - off means no pass, not a pass depositing zero.
+2. **One sprite per bright quarter-res texel** (`HDRFilterPSF`): a procedural triangle list
+   expanded from `SV_VertexID` with no vertex stream at all, six vertices per grid cell. The
+   vertex shader reads its own texel, collapses the quad when nothing survived the threshold -
+   which is what keeps the cost proportional to the number of bright sources rather than to
+   the frame area - and otherwise expands a quad of `Star Size * frame height`, carrying the
+   texel's excess energy. The pixel shader rotates the quad-local coordinate by the plate's
+   angle and samples the PSF image (or the procedural star) through it.
+3. **Composite** additively into `$HDRFinalBloom` with the same bloom-weight divide the
+   streaks and the halation use, so the star's on-screen brightness does not move when the
+   scene's TOD bloom amount does. The deposit is divided by the sprite **area**, which is what
+   makes this a convolution rather than a glow: a wider plate spreads the same energy further
+   instead of getting brighter.
+
+The plate sits in front of the anamorphic element, so its pattern is squeezed horizontally
+along with the rest of the picture (read from `Dof_User_AnamorphicSqueeze` - one knob, not two
+that can drift). It is **not** rotated by the iris phase and **not** scaled by the aperture: it
+is a plate, not the iris.
+
+These are the `HDR_Filter_*` post effect params, owned by `CHDRStreaks` because they are all
+"what the front of the lens does to a point"; an unmodified renderer does not know them.
+Master cvar: `r_HDRStreaks` (0 disables the whole streak / flare / PSF block; **2 does not**
+force the filter on - the plate is separate kit from the iris, and forcing it would hide
+whether the params arrive). Full model in [FilterPSFSpec.md](FilterPSFSpec.md).
+
+---
+
+## Specifications
+
+The design documents behind these features, each one the authority on its own semantics:
+
+- [DofLensModelSpec.md](DofLensModelSpec.md) - the lens model core: thin-lens signed circle of
+  confusion, the shared pupil function with spherical aberration, the energy-weighted
+  scatter-as-gather and the CoC-layered composite.
+- [HybridDofSpec.md](HybridDofSpec.md) - the gather + splat depth of field and the pupil model.
+- [SpriteBokehSpec.md](SpriteBokehSpec.md) - highlight selection and the shape mask.
+- [FieldPupilSpec.md](FieldPupilSpec.md) - the field-dependent pupil: cat-eye, coma and
+  astigmatism, and the rule that the mechanical light loss is applied exactly once.
+- [LensFlawsSpec.md](LensFlawsSpec.md) - field curvature, edge softness and lateral chromatic
+  aberration: what a cheap lens does where it is in focus, at no extra render pass.
+- [AxialChromaticSpec.md](AxialChromaticSpec.md) - longitudinal chromatic aberration: the
+  per-channel disc size that puts green and magenta rims on out-of-focus highlights.
+- [DiffractionStreaksSpec.md](DiffractionStreaksSpec.md) - sunstars, the analytic model.
+- [DiffractionKernelSpec.md](DiffractionKernelSpec.md) - wave-optics sunstars: the FFT of the
+  pupil as the glare point spread function, its far-field extrapolation and spectral sampling.
+- [AnamorphicSpec.md](AnamorphicSpec.md) - squeeze, gate mask and horizontal flare.
+- [HalationSpec.md](HalationSpec.md) - the red-orange film halo.
+- [FilterPSFSpec.md](FilterPSFSpec.md) - the front filter's point spread function: star
+  filters and any other authored front-element PSF, drawn as sparse sprites.
+- [LensGhostSpec.md](LensGhostSpec.md) - internal reflection ghosts, drawn by the sprite bokeh
+  pixel shader because a ghost is an image of the aperture.
+- [ViewfinderSpec.md](ViewfinderSpec.md) - frame guides and focus peaking.
+- [EffectAuthoritySpec.md](EffectAuthoritySpec.md) - who owns the shared render state.
+
+---
+
