+# CinematicCamera Console Reference
+
+Console commands registered by the plugin, plus the renderer cvars the Cinematic Camera system
+relies on (registered in `Code/CryEngine/RenderDll/Common/RendererCVars.cpp`).
+
+The cvars fall into two groups:
+
+- **Live tuning (character)**: values used in normal operation. Note that
+  `r_HDRVignetteAmount`, `r_LensDistortion`, `r_LensChromaticAberration` and `r_LensLetterbox`
+  are **overwritten every
+  frame** while a cinematic camera drives the render state (from its Vignette / Distortion /
+  Anamorphic Squeeze properties), so
+  setting them by hand only sticks while no cinematic camera is active; the plugin restores
+  the pre-activation values when the camera stops driving.
+- **Diagnostic**: force modes used to verify that a pipeline stage runs at all, independent of
+  what the game code feeds it.
+
+Naming standard: every depth-of-field cvar spells the family out as `r_DepthOfField*`. Three
+cvars were renamed to follow it:
+
+| Old name | Current name |
+| --- | --- |
+| `r_DofBokehShapeStrength` | `r_DepthOfFieldBokehShapeStrength` |
+| `r_DofSpriteBokehDebug` | `r_DepthOfFieldSpriteBokehDebug` |
+| `r_DofResolutionScale` | `r_DepthOfFieldResolutionScale` |
+
+(The C++ members keep their short `CV_r_Dof*` names; only the console-facing names changed.)
+
+## Console commands
+
+### ccam_slot
+
+```
+ccam_slot <1-9>
+```
+
+Activates the cinematic camera whose **Camera Slot** property equals the given number, which
+is the same ownership transfer as the Schematyc `Activate` function. It logs the entity it
+switched to (`[CinematicCamera] slot 3 -> 'CamB'`) or reports that no cinematic camera carries
+that slot. Called with no argument it prints its usage line.
+
+A Phone Tracker with **Follow Active Camera** enabled follows every switch, re-anchoring on
+the new camera and re-sending the last lens state to it.
+
+Bind the slots to the number keys to cut between cameras during play:
+
+```
+bind 1 ccam_slot 1
+bind 2 ccam_slot 2
+bind 3 ccam_slot 3
+```
+
+The same switch is exported for other plugins as `CryActivateCinematicCameraSlot(int slot)`
+(see `Interface/ICinematicCameraOptics.h`).
+
+## Live tuning
+
+### r_DepthOfFieldMode
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 1 |
+| Purpose | Selects the DOF implementation. 1 = pure gather. 0 = hybrid: the same gather runs first, then a highlight splat layer (sprite bokeh) is added on top of it. |
+
+Driven by the component's **Use Sprite Bokeh** property (on -> 0), saved and restored around
+the camera being active. Setting it to 0 from the console is an equally valid way to reach the
+hybrid path ??the shape texture follows the effective cvar, not the property.
+
+### r_DepthOfFieldBokehShapeStrength
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 1.0 |
+| Range | input clamped to 0.25-4.0, then the resulting exponent is clamped to 0-1 |
+| Purpose | Reach of the **blade curvature** in the bokeh outline, applied to both the gather taps and the splat's polygon. The iris exponent is `1 - curvature * p * strength`, where `p` is the pupil scale (the opening radius relative to the lens wide open): exponent 0 = perfect circle, 1 = the exact polygon. 1 = physical: a curved blade's arc bulges exactly as far as the opening allows. Above 1 keeps curved blades round further down the stops; below 1 sharpens them into the polygon sooner. |
+
+A real iris opening is the intersection of its blades and is therefore always **convex**, so
+nothing may push its corners past the true polygon. The old 2-4 range did exactly that: the
+radius function is normalised to the edge midpoints, so a corner sits 8% out for an octagon but
+**2.0x** out for a triangle, and an exponent of 2 gave that triangle 4x-radius spikes - a
+concave cloverleaf instead of an aperture.
+
+Not overwritten by the plugin; tune it from the console at any time. Blade Curvature feeds the
+same exponent: straight blades (curvature 0) stay the exact polygon at every aperture, curved
+blades round off in proportion to how far the iris is open. This replaces the old f/1.2 - f/2.8
+formation window, which was an approximation of the same curve for a single fast lens and
+wrongly forced the exact polygon on **every** lens above f/2.8. Curvature's other half is the
+polygon's **edge softness** in the splat (0.06 pupil-radius units when fully curved, 0.015 when
+fully straight), which is what makes a straight blade read as sharper without deforming the
+silhouette.
+
+### r_DepthOfFieldResolutionScale
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 0 |
+| Purpose | Internal resolution of the hybrid splat pass (`r_DepthOfFieldMode 0`). 0 = half-resolution quad grid (cheapest; highlight selection at 2x2 pixel granularity). 1 = native-resolution grid, every native pixel its own splat candidate (sharpest highlight selection; ~4x the DOF vertex work, ~12M vertices at 1080p, early-outs keep the surviving work sparse). 2 = reserved for a native-resolution gather as well; currently behaves as 1. |
+
+Splat energy is normalised per grid tier, so the same Highlight Splat Gain deposits the same
+energy at any of these settings.
+
+### r_HDRVignetteAmount
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 0.0 |
+| Range | clamped to 0.0-4.0 in use |
+| Purpose | Strength of the tone map stage's vignette map. 0 = off (default), 1 = the stock map, 2 = deepened. |
+
+**Overwritten every frame while a cinematic camera is active** (result of Vignette Amount +
+Vignette Aperture Link), in edit mode as well as in game mode.
+
+### r_LensDistortion
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 0.0 |
+| Range | clamped to -0.9-0.9 in use |
+| Purpose | Radial lens distortion in the final composite (PostAA). Positive = barrel, negative = pincushion / wide-angle correction. |
+
+**Overwritten every frame while a cinematic camera is active** (result of Distortion +
+Distortion Focal Link; that result is already multiplied by 0.35, so the written value stays
+within +/-0.35), in edit mode as well as in game mode.
+
+### r_LensChromaticAberration
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 0.0 |
+| Range | clamped to 0-1 in use |
+| Purpose | Lateral chromatic aberration in the final composite (PostAA). Magnification depends on wavelength, so red is sampled at a slightly larger radial scale than green and blue at a slightly smaller one: high-contrast edges pick up a red/orange fringe on the outer side and a blue/cyan one on the inner, zero at the centre and growing toward the frame edges. Applied AFTER the distortion and squeeze remap, so it follows the picture those produce instead of fighting them; green and every other tap in the composite stay on the remapped coordinate. 0 skips the two extra samples entirely. |
+
+**Overwritten every frame while a cinematic camera is active** (the Chromatic Aberration lens
+property, passed through as authored), in edit mode as well as in game mode.
+
+### r_LensLetterbox
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 0.0 |
+| Purpose | Aspect ratio to letterbox the final frame to (PostAA): black bars top and bottom when the frame is wider than the viewport. 0 = off. The visible band is `viewportAspect / letterboxAspect` of the frame height, hard-edged like a real gate mask. A **negative** value is the squeezed presentation (Cinematic Camera "Show Squeezed Frame"): the same band is stretched vertically to fill the viewport instead of masked - the raw anamorphic negative, where the whole picture carries the squeeze. |
+
+**Overwritten every frame while a cinematic camera is active**, in edit mode as well as in game
+mode: the desqueezed anamorphic format aspect (`sensorWidth * Squeeze / sensorHeight`) while
+that format is wider than the viewport, 0 otherwise. Setting it by hand for a plain 2.39:1 mask
+therefore only sticks while no cinematic camera is driving the render state.
+
+### Viewfinder overlays
+
+Nine cvars drive the viewfinder overlays drawn at the end of the PostAA composite. They are
+**overlays: never part of the picture**, and they are **overwritten every frame while a
+cinematic camera is active** (from its Viewfinder property group), in edit mode as well as in
+game mode - so setting them by hand only sticks while no cinematic camera is driving the render
+state. The plugin restores the pre-activation values when the camera stops driving.
+
+| Cvar | Type | Default | Purpose |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `r_LensGuides` | int (bitmask) | 0 | Framing guides: 1 = rule of thirds, 2 = centre cross, 4 = action safe (90%) + title safe (80%) rectangles, 8 = aspect framing guide. 0 = off. |
+| `r_LensGuideAspect` | float | 2.39 | Target aspect for the framing guide (bit 8): the band of that aspect centred in the viewport is outlined and the outside is shaded. |
+| `r_LensGuideOpacity` | float | 0.6 | Line opacity, 0-1. The shading outside the aspect band is half of it. |
+| `r_LensPeaking` | float | 0.0 | Focus peaking strength. Edge response inside the depth of field is tinted with this weight. 0 = off. |
+| `r_LensPeakingNear` | float | 0.0 | Near limit of the in-focus band, metres. Pushed by the component from its thin-lens DOF bounds, every frame. |
+| `r_LensPeakingFar` | float | 0.0 | Far limit of the in-focus band, metres. |
+| `r_LensPeakingColor` | int | 1 | 1 = red, 2 = green, 3 = blue, 4 = yellow, 5 = white. |
+| `r_LensPeakingDebug` | int | 0 | Focus peaking diagnostic. 1 = paint the in-focus band as a solid tint (edge response ignored), 2 = paint the linear scene depth as a grey ramp (black at the camera, white at twice the far limit) with the band tinted. Needs `r_LensPeaking` above 0. |
+| `r_LensOverlays` | int | 1 | Master switch for ALL viewfinder overlays (guides + peaking). |
+
+**`r_LensOverlays 0` is the recording kill switch**: it forces the guide bitmask and the peaking
+strength to zero in the renderer, so no overlay can reach the frame regardless of what the other
+eight cvars or the component's properties say. The component itself sets it to 1 only while
+`gEnv->IsEditing()` is true or its **Overlays In Game Mode** property is on, so a recording made
+in game mode is clean by default.
+
+The near/far limits are pushed every frame whatever the peaking strength, so switching
+`r_LensPeaking` on from the console while a cinematic camera is active finds a real band.
+Without a camera driving them both limits stay 0, which the renderer reads as "no band" and
+widens to the whole depth range rather than tinting nothing.
+
+### r_MotionBlurShutterSpeed
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 250.0 |
+| Purpose | Motion blur exposure time as 1/x seconds. |
+
+Driven by the plugin while the component's **Enable Motion Blur** is on: set to the Camera Body
+`Shutter (1/x s)` denominator (1/50 = the classic 180 degree film look), restored when the
+camera stops driving. `r_MotionBlur` is set to 2 if it was 0.
+
+## Diagnostic
+
+### r_DepthOfFieldBokehQuality
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 0 |
+| Purpose | 0 = normal (bokeh shape driven by the `Dof_User_ApertureBlades` / `Dof_User_FNumber` params). **N >= 3 = force mode**: an N-blade polygonal kernel at full blend and fully curved blades, independent of any game code. 3 (triangle) is the easiest way to verify the kernel shape is live. |
+
+The force mode also keeps the polygon kernel when a shape mask would otherwise take over ??+diagnostics outrank the mask. Kernel parameter changes are logged as
+`[DOF] kernel blades=N fNumber=X`, with `(FORCED)` appended in force mode.
+
+### r_DepthOfFieldSpriteBokehDebug
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 0 |
+| Purpose | Diagnostic for the hybrid sprite bokeh splats (`r_DepthOfFieldMode 0`). 1 = every surviving splat is drawn as its raw shape mask at full strength, skipping the energy normalization, so the silhouette (texture x iris polygon) becomes unmissable. 0 = off. |
+
+### r_DepthOfFieldSpriteBokehFadeCoC
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 1.0 |
+| Purpose | Circle of confusion (gather units; 4 px of disc radius, i.e. 8 px of diameter, per unit at 1080p) at which a hybrid sprite bokeh splat reaches full strength. Splats fade in over one third to one times this value and are skipped below it. The gather's halo loses energy as a highlight approaches focus while an energy-conserving splat only gets brighter per pixel, so without the fade a small sharp silhouette floats on an already-converged light and then vanishes in a single frame. Lower it to keep shapes on smaller discs, raise it if shapes still pop near focus. The splat also sizes itself from the gather's own CoC texture, so both paths agree on how far out of focus a highlight is. |
+
+### r_DepthOfFieldCatEyeBarrel
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 1.05 |
+| Purpose | Tuning for the field-dependent pupil (the **Cat-eye** lens property). Radius of the mechanical barrel opening that clips the pupil off axis, expressed in wide-open pupil radii. 1.0 makes the barrel exactly the wide-open pupil, which is the harshest clipping; above 1 the barrel is wider than the glass, so the corners keep more of their pupil. Shared by the gather, the splat and the tonemap through one derivation, so changing it moves all three together. |
+
+### r_DepthOfFieldCatEyeReach
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 1.0 |
+| Purpose | Tuning for the field-dependent pupil (the **Cat-eye** lens property). How far the barrel's apparent centre slides away from the pupil centre at the frame **corner**, per unit of Cat-eye, in wide-open pupil radii. Raise it to make the cat-eye shape and the mechanical edge darkening reach further in from the edges, lower it to confine them to the extreme corners. |
+
+### r_DepthOfFieldLensModel
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 1 |
+| Purpose | Kill switch for the **lens model** depth of field ([DofLensModelSpec.md](../DofLensModelSpec.md)), which drives focus and blur from the thin lens itself - focal length, f-number, focus distance and sensor - instead of the engine's focus-range ramp, weights the gather by the energy each tap actually scatters onto the pixel, and leaves the depth of field natively sharp. It is a separate shader permutation that runs **only** when the Cinematic Camera component asks for it (`Lens Model DOF`, on by default). 1 = honour the request. 0 = ignore it and always take the stock path, which is then byte for byte the engine's own depth of field - the first thing to try if anything about the blur looks wrong. |
+
+### r_DepthOfFieldLensModelMaxCoC
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 4.0 |
+| Purpose | How far the lens-model **gather kernel** is allowed to reach, in gather units (~4 px of disc radius per unit at 1080p). Not an optical quantity and **not a cap on the lens**: the circle of confusion texture carries the physical blur regardless, and the sprite splats are sized from it - this bounds only the 121-tap half-resolution kernel, which cannot resolve a disc of any size. Past it the gather's halo stops growing and the splat carries the rest. Raising it gives larger halos at the cost of sparser sampling - ringing and visible tap copies in a big disc - which `r_DepthOfFieldLensModelJitter` and the second 3x3 iteration hide. 4.0 is the stock kernel's own reach; ~8 is usable with jitter on. Use `r_DepthOfFieldLensModelDebug 1` to see how far past the cap the lens is actually asking to go. |
+
+### r_DepthOfFieldLensModelSplatMaxCoC
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 256 |
+| Purpose | Largest bokeh sprite the lens-model splat will draw, as a **diameter** in native pixels - 256 means a disc 128 px in radius. It is also the only ceiling the circle of confusion texture takes, so it bounds the physical blur every consumer downstream sees (tile pass, layer alpha, sprite size). A sprite is an *exact* evaluation of the pupil rather than a sparse sample of it, so a large disc costs fill rate and nothing else - hence a cap far above the gather's reach. The consequence to watch for: a very bright point far out of focus shows the gather's capped halo *plus* a larger, fainter exact disc. If that reads wrong, lower this or raise `r_DepthOfFieldLensModelMaxCoC` so the two meet. The stock path is fixed at 64 px and ignores this. |
+
+### r_DepthOfFieldLensModelJitter
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 1.0 |
+| Purpose | How far the lens-model gather may offset each of its 121 taps inside its own cell, as a fraction of the largest offset that still keeps the tap in that cell. A fixed tap grid samples the source at the *same* places in every pixel, so above a few units of reach each tap's bilinear footprint reads as a separate copy of the source and a big disc comes apart into a handful of circles. A per-pixel offset turns that structure into noise, which the temporal AA resolves. The offset is clamped back inside the iris polygon, so only the disc's interior is roughened - the silhouette's outline stays exact. The pattern also advances one golden-ratio step per frame: a jitter that depends on the pixel alone is fixed-pattern noise, and fixed-pattern noise is precisely what temporal AA cannot average away. In the **mask-kernel domain** the amplitude is automatically quartered, because the clamp is a polygon radius and a mask silhouette has none - at full amplitude a tap could be pushed out of the mask's open area and smear the silhouette into the halo. |
+| Values | `0` = the bare stratified grid (brings the tap copies back for an A/B). `0..1` = amplitude, 1 = full. `2` = full amplitude with the per-frame phase **frozen**, i.e. the old static per-pixel pattern - use it to tell tap structure apart from TAA ghosting. |
+
+### r_DepthOfFieldLensModelDebug
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 0 |
+| Purpose | Diagnostic for the lens model. 1 = log the **uncapped** circle of confusion the thin lens is asking for once a second (far at infinity, near at half the focus distance, plus what percentage is being clipped), and paint the composite as a false-colour map: red = how much of the far layer replaced the frame, green = the frame the lens says is sharp, blue = the near layer's pupil opacity. The fastest way to see whether the focus plane, the far ramp and the occlusion agree with each other. 0 = off. |
+
+### r_DepthOfFieldFieldDebug
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 0 |
+| Purpose | Diagnostic for the field-dependent pupil. 1 = the tonemap replaces the picture with the pupil transmission `T` as a grey ramp ??white where the pupil is unclipped, black where the barrel has closed it off. That ramp is exactly the mechanical light loss the vignette applies, so it is the fastest way to see whether Cat-eye and Maximum Aperture are reaching the renderer at all. 0 = off. |
+
+### r_HDRStreaks
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 1 |
+| Purpose | Master switch for the aperture diffraction streaks (sunstars), driven by the `HDR_Streaks_*` post effect params and composited into the bloom target. 0 = off (no passes issued), 1 = on (visibility decided by the game-side params), **2 = diagnostic force mode**: full strength, low threshold (0.5), eight spikes, fixed angle, independent of the game-side params ??use it to verify the streak pass itself runs. |
+
+### r_HDRDiffractionKernel
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 1 |
+| Purpose | Master switch for the **wave-optics** glare kernel: the sunstar of a point source is the Fraunhofer diffraction pattern of the pupil, `|FFT(pupil)|^2`, computed on a job whenever the pupil's shape changes and convolved with the frame's highlights by a sprite pass. 0 = the kernel is never built and the component's `Streak Model: Wave` falls back to the analytic line pairs, with one line in the log ??the kill switch if the pass ever misbehaves. 1 = on. `r_HDRStreaks` is still the master switch above this. |
+
+### r_HDRDiffractionDebug
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 0 |
+| Purpose | Wave-optics glare diagnostics ??four modes that between them make the whole chain visible instead of arguable. 0 = off. |
+| 1 ??kernel | Draws the diffraction kernel itself into the **top-left corner** of the frame on a log tone curve (eight decades of intensity; anything linear is a black square with a white dot). The whole L x L texture is fitted into a square half the frame height. Shows what the FFT actually produced ??ray count, the removed core, the halo an aberrated pupil adds ??without hunting for a lamp bright enough. |
+| 2 ??shape only | The sprite pass runs **exactly as it does normally** (into the glare's own half-resolution layer, which is composited into the frame after the bloom stage) ??same sources, same sprite size, same mip LOD ??but the deposit ignores energy: every source that passed the threshold paints the pattern at full visibility on a **ten-decade map**, `v = (log10 I + 13) / 10`, coloured by the spectral sum renormalised to its own peak. (Mode 1's `log(1 + I*1e6)` is calibrated for the kernel read at level 0; what reaches the sprite is the pixel's footprint mean plus the `1/u^2` tail, `1e-4` down to `1e-12`, which that curve mapped to 0.0007 ??black.) The radial and centre windows still apply: they are geometry, and this mode shows the figure the pass really rasterizes. Answers *does the pass draw at all, where does the pattern land, what is its scale on screen, what shape is it* ??the questions a too-dim result cannot answer. Not a look: everything on screen is the pattern. |
+| 3 ??sources | Draws the pass's own **thresholded and pre-blurred source texture** (the excess above `HDR_Streaks_Threshold`, at half resolution, after the small Gaussian that rounds a lamp's block of texels off ??which is what the sprite grid actually reads) into the same corner, at half the frame height and in the frame's own aspect, on a four-decade grey scale lifted clear of the floor: any excess at all reads 0.25, then E = 1 ??0.30, 10 ??0.44, 100 ??0.62, 1000 ??0.81, 10000 ??1.0. The panel is sampled as a **5x5 max over the source's own texel grid** ??it is a half-size copy of the pass's half-resolution source, so it still minifies 2:1 and a one-texel source was point-sampled away three times out of four ??and it carries a dim floor and a border, so "nothing cleared the threshold" and "the pass never ran" are not the same black rectangle. Once a second it also logs the brightest source on screen, over an asynchronous staging readback (issued one frame, mapped a few frames later ??no stall): `[BLOOM] diffraction sources: max excess = 214.0 (threshold 12.0) at (238, 131) half-res`. If that number is small, no amount of pattern work will make rays appear. |
+| All modes > 0 | The once-per-change `[BLOOM] diffraction glare` log gains a second line with the energy factor (which is now simply `Streak Amount`: the layer is composited straight into the HDR target, so the tone mapper's bloom weight ??printed for reference ??no longer stands between the deposit and the screen), and the sprite's half extent in **native** pixels, so the whole path from a source's excess to a pixel (`E * energy * I * norm`) is readable off the log. |
+
+### r_HDRDiffractionKernelSize
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 512 |
+| Purpose | Grid the pupil is rastered and transformed on: 512 or 256 (anything else snaps to one of the two ??the transform is radix-2). 256 is a **4x cheaper job** at half the structural resolution. The pupil stays half the grid either way, so the pattern's reach on screen is unchanged; only how finely its structure is resolved differs. |
+
+### r_HDRDiffractionSAWaves
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 0.25 |
+| Purpose | Wavefront error, **in waves**, that the lens' `Spherical Aberration` 1 corresponds to in the diffraction kernel. Spherical aberration is a phase error across the pupil (`W = k_sa * this * r^4`), not a loss of light, and that is what puts faint rings and a halo around the core of the pattern. 0 disables the phase term entirely ??a flat pupil, sharp rays, no halo. |
+
+### r_HDRHalation
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 1 |
+| Purpose | Master switch for film halation ??the red-orange halo around sources that overload the emulsion ??driven by the `HDR_Halation_*` post effect params and composited into the bloom target right after the streaks. 0 = off (no passes issued), 1 = on (visibility decided by the game-side params), **2 = diagnostic force mode**: amount 1, threshold 0.5, radius 0.7, independent of the game-side params ??use it to verify the halation pass itself runs. |
+
+## Related post effect params (for reference)
+
+Written by the plugin every frame; not cvars, and not meant to be driven by hand:
+
+- `Dof_Active` (forced to 0 while the component drives DOF, then restored),
+  `Dof_User_Active`, `Dof_User_FocusDistance`, `Dof_User_FocusRange`, `Dof_User_BlurAmount`,
+  `Dof_User_ApertureBlades`, `Dof_User_FNumber`, `Dof_User_BladeRotation`,
+  `Dof_User_BladeCurvature`, `Dof_User_MaskFNumber`, `Dof_User_MaskRotation` (radians,
+  independent of the iris phase), `Dof_User_HighlightThreshold`,
+  `Dof_User_HighlightGain`, `Dof_User_AnamorphicSqueeze` (1 = spherical; above 1 the gather
+  taps and the splat quads are narrowed horizontally, so bokeh becomes vertical ovals)
+- `Dof_User_CatEye`, `Dof_User_PupilScale` (pupil radius relative to the lens wide open,
+  `Maximum Aperture / geometric f-number` clamped to 0.05-1), `Dof_User_Coma`,
+  `Dof_User_Astigmatism` ??the field-dependent pupil; `Dof_User_CatEye` and
+  `Dof_User_PupilScale` are also read by the tonemap, which applies the mechanical light loss
+- `Dof_User_FieldCurvature`, `Dof_User_EdgeSoftness` ??the two lens flaws that are focus
+  errors, applied inside the shared circle-of-confusion math so the downscale, the tile chain
+  and the composite all see the same bent focus
+- `Dof_User_BokehShapeTex` (string param; rewritten every frame so both render command buffer
+  slots converge, unbound on restore)
+- `FilterGrain_Amount` (combined with the TOD environment grain by `max()`)
+- `HDR_Streaks_Active` / `HDR_Streaks_Amount` / `HDR_Streaks_Count` / `HDR_Streaks_Angle` /
+  `HDR_Streaks_Length` (unknown to an unmodified renderer: reads return 0, writes are silently
+  dropped)
+- `HDR_Streaks_AnamorphicAmount` / `HDR_Streaks_AnamorphicLength` /
+  `HDR_Streaks_AnamorphicThreshold` / `HDR_Streaks_AnamorphicTint` (Vec4, rgb) ??the
+  anamorphic horizontal flare. Pushed whenever the camera drives the render state, including
+  while **Enable Diffraction Streaks** is off: the flare belongs to the front element, not to
+  the iris. `r_HDRStreaks` is still the master switch for both.
+- `HDR_Streaks_Mode` (0 = analytic line pairs, 1 = the wave-optics kernel),
+  `HDR_Streaks_WaveAmount` (0..200, default 20: a source radiance boost ??how much brighter the
+  real source is than its clamped HDR value; 1 = the physically exact, nearly invisible answer;
+  no f-number ramp is applied in this mode),
+  `HDR_Streaks_WaveReach` (where the extrapolated far-field tail is cut off, as a fraction of
+  frame height), `HDR_Streaks_Dispersion` (0 = a white pattern at 550 nm). The pupil itself does
+  **not** travel as a post param: the depth of field stage already resolves blades, the polygon
+  exponent, the blade phase, the shape mask and its rotation and scale, the spherical aberration
+  and the geometric f-number every frame, and the bloom stage reads them straight from there so
+  the sunstar and the bokeh can never disagree about the same iris.
+- `HDR_Halation_Amount` / `HDR_Halation_Threshold` / `HDR_Halation_Radius` /
+  `HDR_Halation_Tint` (Vec4, rgb) ??film halation. Overwritten every frame straight from the
+  component's Halation group, exactly like the streak params, whenever the camera drives the
+  render state; nothing here is modulated by the f-number, because halation belongs to the
+  film stock rather than to the lens. Unknown to an unmodified renderer (reads return 0,
+  writes are silently dropped). Master switch: `r_HDRHalation`.
+- `E3DPARAM_HDR_EYEADAPTATION_PARAMS` (exposure) and
+  `E3DPARAM_HDR_COLORGRADING_COLOR_BALANCE` (white balance) are global 3D engine parameters,
+  written from the `UpdateBeforeFinalizeCamera()` hook so the Time of Day system cannot
+  overwrite them in the same frame.
